Q: Fatal error: Uncaught mysqli_sql_exception: A: <p>The issue is you're creating a new database connection inside the loop. You should create one connection and reuse it. The fact that it works locally but fails on production strongly supports an environment/resource difference.</p>
<p><strong>Check the line inside do loop</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<pre><code>$rs_qryCBX01 = mysqli_query(dbconnection(), $sql_qryCBX01);
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>Therefore, if your main query returns 100 rows, you're potentially doing:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>1 main DB connection + 1 connection for row 1<br />
+ 1 connection for row 2 + ...<br />
+ 1 connection for row 19<br />
+ 1 connection for row 20 &lt;-- fails here</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Why did it suddenly start happening?</strong></p>
<p><em><strong>Possibilities include:</strong></em></p>
<ol>
<li>MySQL/MariaDB connection limit changed.</li>
<li>MySQL server configuration changed.</li>
<li>Hosting provider changed resource/process limits.</li>
</ol>
<p>You should create one connection and reuse it.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<pre><code>$conn = dbconnection();

$sql_qryText = &quot;SELECT *,
    DATE_FORMAT($sqlRowDateCreated,'%e/%c/%Y') AS row_date_created
    FROM $sqlTableName
    ORDER BY $sqlRowMainText03 ASC&quot;;

$rs_qryText = mysqli_query($conn, $sql_qryText);

if ($rs_qryText &amp;&amp; mysqli_num_rows($rs_qryText)) {

    while ($row_qryText = mysqli_fetch_assoc($rs_qryText)) {

        // ...

        $sql_qryCBX01 = &quot;SELECT *
            FROM pod_tbl_workers
            WHERE wrk_id = 10&quot;;

        $rs_qryCBX01 = mysqli_query($conn, $sql_qryCBX01);

        if ($rs_qryCBX01 &amp;&amp; mysqli_num_rows($rs_qryCBX01)) {
            $row_qryCBX01 = mysqli_fetch_assoc($rs_qryCBX01);

            echo stripslashes($row_qryCBX01['wrk_last_name']);
            echo ' ';
            echo stripslashes($row_qryCBX01['wrk_first_name']);
        }

        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>

